gaps.proposedFix:
Add a `bytes` field kind, symmetric across modes:
  encode: { "bytes":{ "value":"inputs.token | unhex" } }
  decode: { "bytes":{ "len":"capture.tkl" }, "as":"token" }
`len` accepts any Int expression, including `remaining`. This subsumes the bare `{"value":...}` encode form and is the single most reusable missing piece — every TLV, every length-prefixed blob, every trailing payload needs it.

Add an `until` form and a lookahead primitive:
{ "each":{ "until":"eof || peek(u8) == 0xff", "max":64 }, "as":"options", "fields":[ ... ] }
`peek(<scalarKind>)` reads without consuming; `eof` is true at the frame boundary. `max` keeps the packet bound computable, mirroring §4's `loop:{max:n}` discipline. Encode ignores `until` and iterates the source list.

Allow `as` on bits entries and require the group to be byte-aligned:
{ "bits":[ {"n":2,"as":"ver"}, {"n":2,"as":"type"}, {"n":4,"as":"tkl"} ] }
Validate that the n's sum to a multiple of 8 (CoAP always does), and specify MSB-first ordering explicitly — v0 never states bit order, which is itself an ambiguity.

Allow a predicate as the correlator alongside the named modes:
{ "kind":"expect", "correlate":"capture.messageId == msg.getTemperature.messageId || capture.token == inputs.token", "timeoutMs":2000 }
and let `msg.<name>.<field>` resolve a named message's ENCODED FIELD values, not just its bytes (§6 currently defines msg.<name> as bytes only). The piggybacked/separate branch then falls out of §5's existing `implies`: { "when":"capture.code == 0x00", "state":"awaitingSeparateResponse" }.

Add to §5:
"dedup": { "key":"capture.messageId", "windowMs":247000, "onDuplicate":"replayLastResponse" }
and to the `send` step:
"retry": { "max":4, "initialMs":2000, "factor":2, "jitter":0.5, "giveUp":"error" }
The retry object also bounds the packet count statically, preserving §4's computability guarantee better than a back edge does.

Add "sortBy" to `each` (stable sort, so repeated Uri-Path segments keep source order — which is semantically load-bearing: capture 1's path is /sensors/temperature, not /temperature/sensors), and a general assertion field:
{ "each":"inputs.options", "as":"o", "sortBy":"o.number", "fields":[...] }
{ "assert":"o.number >= prev", "message":"CoAP options must ascend" }
sortBy also makes encode CANONICAL and therefore round-trippable, which it otherwise is not.
